      "text": "*** How to handle frequent driver location update and efficient proximity search on location data\n(Uber have high frequency of write, and also read for update location)\nSolution: Real-time in-memory GeoSpatial Datastore (batch write processing might be good but not ideal)\n-- Using redis, supports geospatial data types and commands, allow handle real-time location update\nwith high throughput and low latency, minimize storage cost with automatic data expiration\n-- Command like GEOADD, GEOSEARCH in Redis offer flexibility and performance\n-- Main challenges for this would be durability due to risk of data loss and system crash. To mitigate\n1. Redis persistence like AOF (append-only file) to periodically save in-memory to disk\n2. Redis sentinel for high availability, provide automatic failover if maser node goes down, replica -> master\n\n*** Manage system overload from frequent driver location\n-- implement adaptive location update intervals, which dynamically adjust frequency of location updates\nbased on contextual factors (speed/direction of travel/proximity, based on ondevice sensors to detect this, etc)\n-- complexity of design algorithm (out of scope for this)\n\n*** prevent multiple ride request from being sent to same drive simuntaneously\n-- Distributed lock with TTL\n-- implemented in Redis, ride request sent to drive, lock is created as unique identifier,\nTTL is set for acceptance window (eg: ~10sec).\n-- if rdier accept within TTL, update rideStatus in DB< lock release in Redis,\n-- If not, then redis lock expires, Matching service find a new ride\n\n*** How to make sure no requests are dropped during peak demand periods\n-- queueing system, FIFO sevring request, system scale horizontally (Kafka)\n-- Kafka allow to commit the offest of the message in the queue only after successfully\nfound the match. If ride service goes down, match request is still in queue waiting to be processed\n\n*** What happen if driver fails to response in timely manner\n-- similar to process workflow logic, can use AWS step function for Temporal.\n-- Temporal workflow would be:\n1. Send the ride request to the first driver\n2. Set a 10-second timeout\n3. If the driver accepts, complete the workflow\n4. If the driver declines or times out, automatically move to the next driver\n5. Continue this process until a driver is found or all drivers are exhausted\n\n*** Further scale system ***\n-- GeoSharding with READ replicas -- scale horizontally adding more servers\n-- using consistent hashing to distribute data across shards,\nand by implementing a replication strategy to ensure that data is replicated across multiple servers.\n\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "*** How to handle frequent driver location update and efficient proximity search on location data\n(Uber have high frequency of write, and also read for update location)\nSolution: Real-time in-memory GeoSpatial Datastore (batch write processing might be good but not ideal)\n-- Using redis, supports geospatial data types and commands, allow handle real-time location update\nwith high throughput and low latency, minimize storage cost with automatic data expiration\n-- Command like GEOADD, GEOSEARCH in Redis offer flexibility and performance\n-- Main challenges for this would be durability due to risk of data loss and system crash. To mitigate\n1. Redis persistence like AOF (append-only file) to periodically save in-memory to disk\n2. Redis sentinel for high availability, provide automatic failover if maser node goes down, replica -> master\n\n*** Manage system overload from frequent driver location\n-- implement adaptive location update intervals, which dynamically adjust frequency of location updates\nbased on contextual factors (speed/direction of travel/proximity, based on ondevice sensors to detect this, etc)\n-- complexity of design algorithm (out of scope for this)\n\n*** prevent multiple ride request from being sent to same drive simuntaneously\n-- Distributed lock with TTL\n-- implemented in Redis, ride request sent to drive, lock is created as unique identifier,\nTTL is set for acceptance window (eg: ~10sec).\n-- if rdier accept within TTL, update rideStatus in DB< lock release in Redis,\n-- If not, then redis lock expires, Matching service find a new ride\n\n*** How to make sure no requests are dropped during peak demand periods\n-- queueing system, FIFO sevring request, system scale horizontally (Kafka)\n-- Kafka allow to commit the offest of the message in the queue only after successfully\nfound the match. If ride service goes down, match request is still in queue waiting to be processed\n\n*** What happen if driver fails to response in timely manner\n-- similar to process workflow logic, can use AWS step function for Temporal.\n-- Temporal workflow would be:\n1. Send the ride request to the first driver\n2. Set a 10-second timeout\n3. If the driver accepts, complete the workflow\n4. If the driver declines or times out, automatically move to the next driver\n5. Continue this process until a driver is found or all drivers are exhausted\n\n*** Further scale system ***\n-- GeoSharding with READ replicas -- scale horizontally adding more servers\n-- using consistent hashing to distribute data across shards,\nand by implementing a replication strategy to ensure that data is replicated across multiple servers.\n\n\n\n",
